  "query_preview": "..\n\nWest coast ridging and a stalled upper level circulation over\nsouthern New Mexico will continue to drive a tightened surface\npressure gradient and easterly winds across southeastern Arizona\nthrough Monday. Wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph will be common with\nstronger winds on high terrain ridges exposed to east winds.\nThough the upper low begins to nudge east out of New Mexico on \nSunday with the western ridge moving over the Four Corners, flow\naloft will remain tight and Sunday's winds may be the strongest\nin the period. Current guidance shows wind gusts exceeding 40\nmph possible on Sunday, especially in the Gila River valley in\nGraham county and southern Cochise county. Temperatures into\nearly next week will remain near to just below normal, with warm\nmornings as increased winds ward off normal radiational \ncooling.\n\nThe building of the ridge next week will warm temperatures to\nnear or just above normal by the middle of the week with \nanother wave potentially bringing increased winds again late in \nthe week. The forecast remains dry at this time, though \nsoutheastern Arizona may be on the periphery of a moisture feed \nfrom Hurricane Rachel next week. Current hurricane ensembles \ntake the track too far to the west to worry about any notable \nimpacts or even precipitation chances at this time, but a \nmodest rise in precipitable water bringing cloud build-ups or a \nvery slight chance of a shower/thunderstorm over high terrain \ncan't be ruled out early next week.",
